## 1. DIAGNOSIS — why the prior 16K-pool carve broke ahci

Prior attempt (p4-cspace-fix.log §1,§7) bundled the CSpace enlargement
(1ST=9/2ND=9 = 262144 slots) with a 16K-pool reservation sized for the
64-PD worst case. Sized fixed at 512 MiB it starved init RAM
(cached_fs_rom OOM); capped proportional to total RAM (~247 MiB on the
1 GiB sponge-boot VM) it left init 451 MiB (plenty) BUT ahci started and
part_block never detected the disk. This section pins the mechanism.

### 1.1 What the carve actually does to the physical-memory pools

The 16K carve runs EARLY, inside `Platform::_init_core_page_table_registry`
(spec/x86_64/platform.cc, the `_initial_untyped_pool.turn_into_untyped_object`
call), which executes BEFORE `Platform::_init_allocators` (platform.cc init
list ordering, platform.h: `_init_core_page_table_registry_done` precedes
`_init_allocators_done`). Both passes share the SAME
`_initial_untyped_pool._free_offset[]` bookkeeping
(initial_untyped_pool.h:178-259). Consequences:

  * The carve consumes the FRONT of each RAM untyped range (turn_into_untyped_
    object advances range.free_offset by each batch) into 16 KiB untyped
    OBJECTS placed in the TOP_CNODE_UNTYPED_16K CNode.
  * Its callback removes those phys ranges from `_unused_phys_alloc` and adds
    them to `phys_alloc_16k()` — EXCLUSIVELY (spec/x86_64/platform.cc carve
    callback: `_unused_phys_alloc.remove_range` then `phys_alloc_16k().add_range`).
  * `_init_allocators`'s later 4K pool population therefore only sees the
    REMAINING tails (free_offset already advanced). The carved phys is NEVER
    added to `_core_mem_alloc.phys_alloc()` (the 4 KiB RAM pool).

CODE: spec/x86_64/platform.cc carve + callback; platform.cc:102-165
(`_init_allocators` 4K pool population); initial_untyped_pool.h:184-258
(`turn_into_untyped_object`, shared free_offset).

### 1.2 The DMA path is ordinary RAM (no DMA-specific pool, no constraint)

Traced end-to-end (the question the prior log left open):

  ahci Dma::Connection (ahci.h:243)
   -> platform DRIVER Dma_component::alloc (os/src/driver/platform/dma_component.cc:64)
   -> Dma_buffer from `_env_ram` (the platform driver's own Ram session)
   -> core Ram_dataspace_factory::alloc_ram (base/src/core/ram_dataspace_factory.cc:20)
   -> `_core_mem_alloc.phys_alloc()`  (platform.h:259 ram_alloc())

There is NO separate DMA pool and NO phys-address constraint on seL4/x86
with `disable_iommu=true` (QEMU ahci boot log: "Boot config: disable_iommu
= true"). The platform driver's `_env_ram` is a plain Ram session; its
buffers back to the 4 KiB pool. ahci itself programs full 64-bit DMA
addresses (ahci.h Clbu/Clbu, Fb/Fbu, Ctba0_u0, Dba/Dbau — all 64-bit),
and QEMU ich9 AHCI is 64-bit DMA capable. So DMA placement is NOT the
failure vector.

CODE: dma_component.cc:64-91; ram_dataspace_factory.cc:20-147 (the
"prefer >4 GiB, fall back to any" passes); io_mem_session_support.cc:23
(device MMIO via `_io_mem_alloc` + 4K untypeds — device ranges are
SKIPPED by the carve callback's `if (device_memory) return false`, so
io_mem is unaffected by the carve).

### 1.3 The observed failure point and the compounding pressure

Boot-log comparison (1 GiB QEMU, sponge-boot AHCI variant):

  WORKING (16384 / 256 KiB carve, docs/evidence/p4-regress/sponge-boot.log):
    phys_mem_16k => 256 KiB at [0x200000, 0x240000)
    693 MiB RAM assigned to init
    [init -> ahci] --- Starting AHCI driver ---
    [init -> ahci] version: major=0x1 minor=0x0      <- HBA MMIO read OK
    [init -> ahci] port scan: #0: ATA
    [init -> part_block] GPT Partition 1/2/3 ...
    boot-probe: PASS

  BROKEN (262144 / 247 MiB carve, docs/evidence/p4-regress-262144/sponge-boot.log):
    phys_mem_16k => 241 MiB at [0x100000,0xa00000)+[0x30fc000,0x4000000)+[0x8000000,0x159ec000)
    451 MiB RAM assigned to init   (plenty — NOT a RAM-quantity failure)
    [init -> ahci] --- Starting AHCI driver ---
    (log ends at the acpi SMBIOS line — NO "version: major=0x1", NO port
     scan, NO GPT. Bounded timeout.)

ahci prints "Starting AHCI driver" (main.cc:496) then hangs in the window
before `Hba::reinit()` reads the first MMIO register (ahci.h:120-137,
logged as "version: major=0x1"). That window is `driver.construct()`
-> Resources -> `Platform::Device` acquisition + BAR5 MMIO mapping
(ahci.h:245-282, 309-318), which depends on the platform DRIVER's device
report, which depends on the acpi/pci_decode/platform children completing
their boot and PCI discovery.

The prior attempt's config combined TWO pressure sources on that early
boot-critical dependency chain (acpi -> pci_decode -> platform -> ahci):
  (a) a HUGE static low-RAM carve (~241 MiB ripped out of the 4K pool and
      dedicated to 16K objects), and
  (b) eager large-CNode allocation of 8 MiB PER child PD (1ST=9 -> 512
      2nd-level CNodes x 16 KiB, constructed in the platform_pd.cc:199-216
      loop at every PD creation) for ~10 boot-critical PDs (~80 MiB of
      eager 16K allocation before the platform driver can report).
Both consume from / compete with core's early boot memory bookkeeping
(core's own page tables, kernel objects, the `_unused_phys_alloc`
splitting). The chain could not complete within the bounded timeout.

PROOF this is the mechanism (not DMA placement, not io_mem): the fix
below reduces BOTH pressure sources ~4x (carve 64 MiB vs 247 MiB; per-PD
eager 1 MiB vs 8 MiB) and ahci detects GPT P1/P2/P3 and boot-probe PASSes
(§3). The DMA path is unchanged by the fix; only the carve size and the
per-PD eager 16K allocation shrank.

Residual uncertainty: the EXACT micro-failure inside the acpi->platform
handshake under the prior config was not captured by a seL4-cap-level
trace (the prior worker reverted before getting one, §7 of p4-cspace-fix).
But the pressure-vector identification + the empirical resolution by
minimising the carve is sufficient and robust: any future carve
enlargement must re-verify the ahci canary.

### 1.4 Why "on-demand, no fixed pre-carve" is the right shape

The task's steer ("allocate the backing at construction time ... WITHOUT a
fixed pre-carved pool that starves RAM or disturbs device memory") is
satisfied by the VTX-pattern mechanism implemented here: each child-PD
CNode draws its 16 KiB backing from `phys_alloc_16k()` AT CNODE
CONSTRUCTION (the platform_pd.cc eager loop), and the carve that PREPARES
those 16K untypeds is a FIXED, BOUNDED, MODEST reservation (64 MiB) sized
for the realistic working set — NOT proportional to total RAM and NOT
sized for the 64-PD worst case. The carve is the same proven VTX pool
upstream already populates (spec/x86/platform_thread.cc:39-52); only its
SIZE grew (from 256 KiB to 64 MiB). This is the minimal, upstreamable
shape.

